(function(){
const A=window.NLDG_APPLY_CURRENT_EVENTS_V2;if(!A)return;

A({week:20,title:'Privacy and Surveillance',summary:'God’s complete knowledge is loving, holy, and personal. Human data collection is limited and morally accountable, so Christians should care about privacy, consent, security, truth, and the power created by information.',scripture:['Psalm 139:1–12','Proverbs 11:13','Luke 12:2–3','Philippians 2:3–4'],memoryVerse:'Psalm 139:1–3',bigIdea:'“God sees everything” is not a blank check for people or institutions to collect everything. God’s omniscience belongs to His unique character; human surveillance must be judged by purpose, consent, proportionality, security, justice, and neighbor-love.',objectives:['Distinguish divine omniscience from human surveillance.','Recognize privacy as connected to dignity, trust, and power.','Evaluate data collection by purpose and proportionality.','Practice better stewardship of other people’s information.','Choose one privacy or security habit to improve.'],openingQuestion:'If information is useful, does that automatically mean someone has the right to collect it?',sections:[{heading:'God Knows Us Completely',type:'scripture',content:'Psalm 139 describes God’s knowledge as inescapable and intimate. The psalmist responds with wonder because the One who knows him is also Creator, covenant Lord, and faithful presence.'},{heading:'God’s Omniscience Is Not Human Permission',type:'clarity',content:'Human beings are limited, sinful, and capable of misuse. A company, government, church, parent, employer, or platform cannot claim God-like entitlement to unlimited knowledge about people.'},{heading:'Information Creates Power',type:'discernment',content:'Data can improve safety, services, medicine, accessibility, and convenience. It can also enable manipulation, discrimination, exposure, stalking, fraud, or control. Christians should ask who collects information, why, how long it is kept, who can access it, and who bears the risk.'},{heading:'Confidentiality Is Neighbor-Love',type:'application',content:'Proverbs warns against betraying confidence. Pastoral care, friendship, employment, health information, family stories, photographs, and private messages deserve careful stewardship. Curiosity is not consent.'},{heading:'Jesus Calls Hidden Things into Truth',type:'christ',content:'Christian privacy is not a right to conceal abuse, exploitation, fraud, or danger. Scripture also values truth and accountability. Healthy privacy protects dignity; secrecy can protect wrongdoing. Wisdom learns the difference.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Review one account, app, device, or ministry practice. Reduce unnecessary permissions, strengthen account security, avoid sharing another person’s story without permission, and ask whether the information collected is truly needed.'}],questions:['How is God’s knowledge different from human surveillance?','Why does information create power?','When does privacy protect dignity, and when can secrecy protect wrongdoing?','What information about other people do you hold that requires care?','How should churches handle confidential prayer or pastoral information?','What privacy practice can you improve this week?'],prayer:'God who knows us fully and loves us faithfully, teach us to handle information with humility. Protect people from exploitation and exposure. Give leaders wisdom to collect only what is needed, secure what is entrusted, and use knowledge to serve rather than control. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Psalm 139 as relational theology',content:'The psalm celebrates God’s intimate knowledge and presence rather than presenting surveillance as an abstract power.'},{heading:'Confidential speech',content:'Wisdom literature treats the ability to keep confidence as a mark of trustworthy character.'},{heading:'Modern scale',content:'Digital systems can combine location, purchases, communications, biometrics, and behavior at a scale unavailable in the ancient world, increasing both usefulness and moral risk.'}],theology:['Omniscience: complete knowledge belongs uniquely to God.','Dignity: people are not merely data points.','Truth: privacy cannot justify hiding harm.','Stewardship: information entrusted to us carries responsibility.','Justice: surveillance burdens can fall unevenly on vulnerable communities.'],worldview:{foundation:'Information should serve human dignity and legitimate purposes rather than become unchecked power.',affirm:'Responsible data use can improve safety, accessibility, healthcare, fraud prevention, and public service.',caution:'Convenience can normalize unnecessary collection, weak consent, permanent retention, profiling, or unequal monitoring.',response:'Use proportional collection, meaningful consent where possible, strong security, limited retention, transparent purpose, and accountable oversight.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Do Christians have a right to privacy if God sees everything?',response:'God’s knowledge does not erase appropriate human boundaries. Scripture recognizes confidentiality, private prayer, private property, family life, and limits on other people’s access.'},{question:'Can surveillance be used for safety?',response:'Yes, but legitimate safety goals still require proportionality, accuracy, oversight, limits, and attention to unintended harms.'},{question:'Should churches collect member data?',response:'Only what serves a clear ministry purpose, with appropriate notice, security, access controls, retention limits, and special care for sensitive pastoral or child information.'}],misunderstandings:['If you have nothing to hide, privacy does not matter.','God’s omniscience justifies unlimited human monitoring.','Privacy always means secrecy.','Data is neutral regardless of who controls it or how it is used.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Use strong unique passwords and multi-factor authentication.','Ask before posting another person’s private story or image.'],family:['Set age-appropriate privacy boundaries for children.','Avoid using monitoring as a substitute for relationship and trust.'],church:['Limit access to pastoral, donor, child, and volunteer data.','Create clear retention and safeguarding practices.'],community:['Support transparent oversight of high-impact surveillance systems.','Consider how data practices affect people with less power.']}});

A({week:21,title:'Escapism vs. Eternal Hope',summary:'Christian hope looks toward God’s future without abandoning the responsibilities of the present. Eternal perspective should make us more faithful here, not less engaged.',scripture:['Colossians 3:1–4','Acts 1:6–11','1 Corinthians 15:58','Revelation 21:1–5'],memoryVerse:'1 Corinthians 15:58',bigIdea:'Setting our minds on things above does not mean ignoring earth. Because Christ is risen and God promises renewal, believers can face pain honestly, do meaningful work, and refuse both despair and escapism.',objectives:['Distinguish eternal hope from avoidance of present responsibility.','Read Colossians 3 as a call to transformed earthly living.','Recognize spiritualized forms of escapism.','Connect resurrection hope with faithful work.','Choose one present responsibility hope empowers you to face.'],openingQuestion:'When does “God will work it out” become faith, and when can it become a way of avoiding something we need to face?',sections:[{heading:'Things Above Lead to Life Below',type:'scripture',content:'Colossians 3 does not end with private heavenly thoughts. The chapter immediately moves into sexuality, anger, truthfulness, forgiveness, relationships, work, and worship. Heavenly identity produces earthly obedience.'},{heading:'The Disciples Were Sent Back to Their Mission',type:'christ',content:'In Acts 1 the disciples look toward heaven after Jesus’ ascension. The angelic question redirects them toward the mission Jesus gave. Hope in His return is not permission to stand still.'},{heading:'Escapism Can Wear Spiritual Language',type:'discernment',content:'We can use prophecy, entertainment, busyness, prayer language, or “letting go” to avoid grief, conflict, justice, finances, health, relationships, or repentance. Avoidance often feels peaceful briefly while allowing problems to deepen.'},{heading:'Resurrection Makes Present Work Matter',type:'teaching',content:'First Corinthians 15 ends the great resurrection chapter by saying our labor in the Lord is not in vain. Christian hope gives present faithfulness weight because God’s future is renewal, not meaninglessness.'},{heading:'Hope Makes Room for Grief',type:'clarity',content:'Hope does not require pretending loss is small. Revelation’s promise that God will wipe away tears assumes there are real tears to wipe away. Christian hope faces suffering while refusing to believe suffering is final.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Name one responsibility, conversation, grief, or problem you have been avoiding. Ask what one faithful step is possible now, take it, and place the outcome in God’s hands rather than confusing surrender with inaction.'}],questions:['What does Colossians 3 do immediately after telling believers to seek things above?','How can spiritual language become a form of avoidance?','Why does resurrection make present work matter?','What is the difference between surrender and passivity?','How does Christian hope make room for grief?','What present responsibility is hope helping you face?'],prayer:'Risen Jesus, keep our hope from becoming escape. Give us courage to face what is real, patience in suffering, faithfulness in work, and confidence that nothing done in You is wasted. Help us live today in light of the future You promise. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Colossians and hidden life',content:'Believers’ lives are “hidden with Christ,” but Paul immediately describes transformed conduct in ordinary relationships.'},{heading:'Acts and waiting',content:'The early church waits for the Spirit by gathering, praying, choosing leadership, and preparing for mission rather than abandoning ordinary responsibility.'},{heading:'Resurrection hope',content:'First Corinthians 15 connects future bodily resurrection with steadfast present service.'}],theology:['Hope: God’s promised future gives meaning to present faithfulness.','Resurrection: Christian hope is embodied renewal, not escape from creation.','Mission: expectation of Christ’s return energizes witness.','Lament: hope and grief can coexist.','Stewardship: present responsibilities matter because creation belongs to God.'],worldview:{foundation:'The future belongs to God, so believers neither worship the present age nor abandon it.',affirm:'Rest, entertainment, imagination, and temporary relief can be healthy gifts when they restore rather than replace responsibility.',caution:'Escapism can hide behind spirituality, endless media, conspiracy, fantasy, overwork, or obsession with future events.',response:'Face reality, grieve honestly, act faithfully, rest appropriately, and anchor hope in resurrection and new creation.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is longing for heaven escapist?',response:'Not necessarily. Biblical longing for God’s future can deepen courage and faithfulness. It becomes escapist when it consistently replaces responsibilities God has placed before us.'},{question:'Should Christians focus on fixing the world if Jesus will return?',response:'Yes, in the sense of faithful witness, justice, mercy, stewardship, and service. We do not imagine we can create the kingdom by human power, but we also do not abandon neighbors while waiting for Christ.'},{question:'Can entertainment be escapism?',response:'It can be healthy recreation. It becomes unhealthy when used compulsively to avoid grief, duties, relationships, or necessary decisions.'}],misunderstandings:['Eternal perspective means earthly problems do not matter.','Surrender means doing nothing.','Hope requires positive feelings.','Concern for justice or creation distracts from heaven.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Identify one avoidance pattern and one faithful next step.','Use entertainment as rest rather than anesthesia.'],family:['Talk about hard realities without hopelessness.','Follow prayer with appropriate action when action is available.'],church:['Teach end-times hope in ways that strengthen service.','Avoid prophecy teaching that feeds fear or neglect of present responsibilities.'],community:['Stay engaged in long-term needs even when results are slow.','Practice hope through durable acts of service.']}});

A({week:22,title:'Gender and Biblical Identity',summary:'Christian teaching about human identity begins with creation, embodiment, the image of God, and belonging to Christ. Conviction about sex and gender must be joined to truthfulness, humility, pastoral care, and the dignity of every person.',scripture:['Genesis 1:26–28','Psalm 139:13–18','Matthew 19:4–6','Colossians 3:1–4'],memoryVerse:'Genesis 1:27',bigIdea:'Historic Christian teaching understands our bodies as meaningful gifts rather than accidental containers. At the same time, no person should be reduced to a gender question; every person is an image-bearer invited into the grace and lordship of Jesus.',objectives:['Ground identity first in God’s creation and grace.','Explain the historic Christian understanding of male and female embodiment.','Distinguish theological conviction from stereotypes about masculinity and femininity.','Practice pastoral language that protects dignity and refuses mockery.','Recognize that discipleship involves the whole person, including the body.'],openingQuestion:'How can Christians hold convictions about the body and still make sure a person never feels reduced to one disputed issue?',sections:[{heading:'Begin with Image-Bearing',type:'scripture',content:'Genesis first says humanity is created in God’s image. Male and female are named within that shared dignity. Whatever questions follow, neither sex possesses greater worth, spiritual access, or image-bearing status.'},{heading:'The Body Is Part of the Person God Loves',type:'teaching',content:'Biblical faith does not treat the body as disposable packaging around the “real self.” Creation, incarnation, resurrection, and Christian sexual ethics all give the body moral and theological significance.'},{heading:'Male and Female Are Not Stereotype Boxes',type:'clarity',content:'Scripture’s affirmation of male and female does not require every man or woman to fit cultural stereotypes about personality, hobbies, clothing styles, emotions, work, or temperament. Christians should not confuse social expectations with biblical commands.'},{heading:'Jesus Holds Creation and Compassion Together',type:'christ',content:'Jesus cites the creation story in teaching about marriage while consistently treating people who were socially vulnerable or morally complicated with dignity. Christian conviction should sound like Jesus rather than ridicule or disgust.'},{heading:'Discipleship Is More Than Self-Definition',type:'discernment',content:'Christian identity is received under Christ’s lordship rather than built from desire, achievement, pain, politics, or social approval. That principle applies to every believer. The church should resist both self-invention and the temptation to define another person only by one struggle.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Examine your language about people whose gender experience or views differ from yours. Remove mockery, contempt, stereotypes, and careless assumptions. Practice speaking your conviction in a way that you could say while looking the person in the eyes.'}],questions:['What does Genesis say before it says male and female?','Why does Christian theology give the body significance?','Which gender stereotypes come more from culture than Scripture?','How did Jesus combine moral conviction with dignity toward people?','Why is no person reducible to one area of disagreement?','What would truthful and compassionate speech require from you?'],prayer:'Creator God, thank You for making people in Your image and for taking human flesh in Jesus. Give us wisdom to honor the body, humility where questions are difficult, and compassion toward every person. Remove mockery and fear from our speech. Form all of us under the gracious lordship of Christ. Amen.',leaderReminder:'Teach the church’s conviction without turning transgender or gender-nonconforming people into objects of debate. Speak as though someone personally affected is in the room, because they may be.',background:[{heading:'Creation and shared dignity',content:'Genesis 1 presents humanity as male and female within the singular vocation of image-bearing and stewardship. The text gives equal dignity before later social roles are discussed.'},{heading:'Embodiment in Christian theology',content:'Christian faith values bodies through creation, incarnation, bodily resurrection, baptism, the Lord’s Supper, and commands concerning embodied life.'},{heading:'Pastoral complexity',content:'Contemporary discussions distinguish biological sex, gender roles, identity, dysphoria, social expression, and medical questions. These categories should not be collapsed into one simple claim.'}],theology:['Creation: bodies belong to God’s good creation.','Image of God: every person has equal dignity.','Incarnation: the Son of God took a human body.','Sanctification: discipleship includes embodied desires and practices.','Grace: Christian teaching never removes the call to patience, compassion, and belonging.'],worldview:{foundation:'Historic Christian and Nazarene teaching understands human embodiment and the male-female creation pattern as morally meaningful under God’s design.',affirm:'People can resist restrictive cultural stereotypes, seek wise pastoral and professional support, and ask difficult questions without losing dignity.',caution:'Churches can cause harm through mockery, oversimplification, public shaming, careless amateur diagnosis, or treating political talking points as pastoral care.',response:'Teach convictions clearly, listen carefully, protect dignity and safety, distinguish different kinds of questions, and keep relationship and discipleship centered on Jesus.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does rejecting stereotypes weaken belief in male and female?',response:'No. A biological or theological claim about sex does not require narrow cultural rules about personality, interests, vocation, clothing preferences, or emotional style.'},{question:'How should a church respond to someone experiencing gender dysphoria?',response:'With patience, confidentiality within safeguarding limits, truthful teaching, careful listening, family and pastoral support, and referral to appropriately qualified professionals when clinical care is needed. Do not shame, mock, or force public disclosure.'},{question:'What about names and pronouns?',response:'Christians disagree about how conscience and pastoral accommodation apply. Leaders should avoid turning every interaction into a public confrontation, clarify their own convictions, consider context and relationship, and refuse humiliating or demeaning treatment.'}],misunderstandings:['Biblical teaching requires rigid personality stereotypes for men and women.','A person asking gender questions has rejected God and cannot belong in church.','Mockery is an acceptable defense of truth.','Every contemporary gender-related question is identical and requires the same pastoral response.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Remove demeaning jokes and labels from your speech.','Learn enough to distinguish sex, stereotypes, identity questions, and clinical distress.'],family:['Create space for questions without panic or public shame.','Teach children that dignity is never earned by fitting stereotypes.'],church:['Develop pastoral and safeguarding practices before a crisis occurs.','Teach embodiment within the larger gospel rather than only in reaction to controversy.'],community:['Defend people from bullying or violence even amid disagreement.','Participate in public conversations without denying anyone’s humanity.']}});

A({week:23,title:'Sexuality and Holiness',summary:'Christian sexual ethics belongs inside a larger call to holiness for every believer. The church must reject double standards, shame, exploitation, and the idea that sexual desire defines a person’s entire identity.',scripture:['1 Thessalonians 4:1–8','1 Corinthians 6:12–20','Matthew 5:27–30','Galatians 5:13–25'],memoryVerse:'1 Thessalonians 4:3–4',bigIdea:'Holiness means our bodies, desires, relationships, and choices belong to Jesus. Sexual ethics is not a special burden for one group; every Christian is called to chastity, self-control, truth, and love appropriate to their state of life.',objectives:['Place sexual ethics within whole-life holiness.','Reject double standards based on gender, orientation, marital status, or reputation.','Distinguish desire, temptation, identity, and chosen behavior.','Connect boundaries with love rather than disgust or fear.','Practice grace and accountability without public shame.'],openingQuestion:'Why does the church sometimes speak as though sexual holiness is mainly about other people’s sins instead of every disciple’s body and desires?',sections:[{heading:'Paul Begins with Pleasing God',type:'scripture',content:'First Thessalonians 4 places sexual conduct inside a larger life of sanctification. Holiness is not obsession with sex; it is the whole person increasingly ordered toward God in love and obedience.'},{heading:'The Body Belongs to the Lord',type:'teaching',content:'First Corinthians 6 rejects both shame about the body and the idea that bodily choices are spiritually irrelevant. Christian freedom includes responsibility because the body is part of the person Christ redeems.'},{heading:'Desire Is Not the Whole Self',type:'clarity',content:'People experience desires they did not simply choose. Temptation, attraction, fantasy, and behavior are related but not identical. Christian discipleship should avoid collapsing a person’s entire identity into one pattern of desire.'},{heading:'Holiness Requires One Standard of Dignity',type:'discernment',content:'Churches lose credibility when they condemn one group’s sexual behavior while excusing adultery, pornography, coercion, harassment, abuse, or exploitative relationships among socially accepted members. Holiness begins with the household of faith.'},{heading:'Jesus Moves the Question toward the Heart',type:'christ',content:'In Matthew 5 Jesus refuses a merely external sexual ethic. He addresses the inner gaze that turns another person into an object. Christian purity is not fear of bodies; it is learning to see and treat people as neighbors rather than consumable experiences.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Identify one sexual or relational boundary that would strengthen faithfulness, whether in media, messaging, dating, marriage, fantasy, secrecy, or accountability. Take one concrete step without using shame as motivation.'}],questions:['How does Paul connect sexuality with sanctification?','Why does the body matter in Christian discipleship?','What is the difference between temptation and chosen behavior?','Where do churches practice sexual double standards?','How does objectification violate neighbor-love?','What boundary would make your own life more truthful and faithful?'],prayer:'Holy God, our bodies and desires belong before You. Give us purity without pride, self-control without shame, compassion without compromise, and courage to confess what is hidden. Help us honor every person as an image-bearer rather than an object. Form our sexuality under the lordship of Jesus. Amen.',leaderReminder:'Keep the lesson universal. Do not single out LGBTQ people as though heterosexual Christians stand outside the call to sexual holiness.',background:[{heading:'Thessalonian holiness',content:'Paul teaches sexual self-control within a young Christian community learning a new way of life in a Greco-Roman environment with different sexual norms.'},{heading:'Corinth and the body',content:'First Corinthians responds to slogans that separated spiritual freedom from bodily conduct. Paul grounds ethics in belonging to Christ.'},{heading:'Jesus and lust',content:'Matthew 5 moves beyond technical avoidance of adultery toward the heart’s treatment of another person, confronting objectification.'}],theology:['Sanctification: grace transforms desires, habits, and relationships over time.','Embodiment: bodily choices matter spiritually.','Chastity: every Christian is called to sexual faithfulness appropriate to marriage or singleness.','Grace: temptation is not identical to condemnation.','Justice: coercion, abuse, exploitation, and harassment contradict holiness.'],worldview:{foundation:'Sexuality is a meaningful part of embodied human life but is not the supreme source of identity or freedom.',affirm:'Affection, intimacy, covenant, attraction, pleasure, and family can be good gifts when ordered toward faithful love and responsibility.',caution:'Both permissive and shame-based cultures can commodify bodies, hide abuse, and confuse desire with entitlement.',response:'Practice chastity, consent, honesty, fidelity, self-control, accountability, and care for people harmed by sexual sin or exploitation.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is having an unwanted sexual attraction itself a sin?',response:'Christian traditions often distinguish temptation or involuntary attraction from chosen lust, fantasy, or behavior. Pastoral care should avoid condemning people for the mere presence of a temptation they are trying to navigate faithfully.'},{question:'What does the Church of the Nazarene teach about sexual relationships?',response:'Historic Nazarene teaching locates sexual intimacy within the covenant of marriage between a man and a woman and calls all people to holiness, fidelity, chastity, and grace.'},{question:'How should the church respond after sexual sin?',response:'With truth, repentance, accountability, appropriate boundaries, restoration where possible, and protection of anyone harmed. Grace is not secrecy, and forgiveness does not eliminate safeguarding or legal responsibilities.'}],misunderstandings:['Sexual holiness is mainly an LGBTQ issue.','Temptation and chosen behavior are identical.','Shame produces lasting holiness.','Forgiveness means abuse or coercion should remain private.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Use honest accountability rather than secrecy.','Set digital and relational boundaries before temptation peaks.'],family:['Teach sexuality with dignity, consent, responsibility, and grace rather than fear alone.','Keep age-appropriate communication open.'],church:['Apply moral standards consistently across leaders and members.','Follow safeguarding and reporting requirements for abuse or exploitation.'],community:['Support survivors without forcing reconciliation.','Resist sexualized media practices that dehumanize people.']}});

A({week:24,title:'Pornography and Purity',summary:'Pornography trains people to consume bodies, secrecy, fantasy, and novelty. Christian purity is not disgust toward sexuality but a reordering of desire toward truth, dignity, covenant, and self-control.',scripture:['Matthew 5:27–30','1 Corinthians 6:18–20','Job 31:1','Philippians 4:8'],memoryVerse:'Matthew 5:28',bigIdea:'Jesus addresses the heart that turns another person into an object. Freedom from pornography usually requires more than guilt; it requires truth, changed access, accountability, healing, and new habits of seeing people as image-bearers.',objectives:['Understand purity as love and integrity rather than shame.','Recognize how pornography can shape desire and relationships.','Identify practical environmental boundaries.','Respond to relapse with repentance and renewed action rather than hopelessness.','Protect children and vulnerable people from sexual exploitation.'],openingQuestion:'Why is secrecy such a powerful part of pornography’s hold on people?',sections:[{heading:'Jesus Moves Beyond External Compliance',type:'scripture',content:'Matthew 5 confronts lustful looking that reduces another person to an object of consumption. Jesus’ strong language communicates the seriousness of cutting off patterns that repeatedly lead toward harm.'},{heading:'Purity Is Not Disgust Toward the Body',type:'clarity',content:'Christian faith calls the body God’s creation and, for believers, a temple of the Holy Spirit. Purity is not learning to hate sexuality; it is learning to honor bodies, desires, covenants, and people truthfully.'},{heading:'Pornography Trains a Way of Seeing',type:'discernment',content:'Repeated sexualized media can train secrecy, novelty-seeking, comparison, objectification, and unrealistic expectations. It can affect singles and married people, men and women, teenagers and adults.'},{heading:'Shame Hides What Grace Brings into the Light',type:'christ',content:'Jesus calls people to repentance without declaring them beyond hope. Shame says “Hide because this is who you are.” Grace says “Tell the truth because this does not have to master you.”'},{heading:'Freedom Needs Practical Architecture',type:'application',content:'Prayer matters, and so do device placement, filters, accountability, removing private access, changing routines, counseling when needed, addressing loneliness or trauma, and refusing the fantasy that willpower alone is always enough.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'If pornography is part of your life, tell one trustworthy mature person and change one point of access today. If it is not, review how your media habits shape the way you look at people and strengthen safeguards for children or vulnerable users.'}],questions:['What kind of looking does Jesus confront?','Why is purity different from shame about the body?','How can pornography shape expectations beyond the moment of viewing?','Why does secrecy strengthen compulsive behavior?','What practical changes support repentance?','How can churches discuss pornography without humiliating people?'],prayer:'Jesus, bring hidden things into Your healing light. Teach us to see people as persons, not objects. Give courage for confession, wisdom for boundaries, healing for those harmed by exploitation, and perseverance for anyone seeking freedom. Make our desires truthful, loving, and holy. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Matthew 5 and the heart',content:'Jesus intensifies sexual ethics by addressing intention and objectification, not merely the legal boundary of adultery.'},{heading:'Corinth and embodied belonging',content:'Paul grounds sexual conduct in the body’s belonging to Christ and rejects using another person as an object of appetite.'},{heading:'Modern pornography',content:'Digital access increases availability, anonymity, novelty, and exposure. Experiences vary, so leaders should avoid unsupported universal claims while taking potential harms seriously.'}],theology:['Purity: sexual holiness is positive love and integrity, not mere avoidance.','Dignity: people must never be reduced to consumable images.','Grace: confession opens the way to change rather than permanent shame.','Justice: pornography can involve exploitation, coercion, trafficking, and nonconsensual distribution.','Sanctification: freedom often develops through sustained practices and community.'],worldview:{foundation:'Sexual desire should serve covenant love and human dignity rather than consumption.',affirm:'Sexual desire itself is not dirty; Scripture places it within embodied, faithful relationships.',caution:'Easy access, secrecy, commercial incentives, and algorithmic escalation can make harmful patterns difficult to interrupt.',response:'Tell the truth, reduce access, seek accountability and qualified help when needed, protect vulnerable people, and cultivate non-objectifying relationships.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is every use of pornography an addiction?',response:'No. Some use is occasional, some compulsive, and some may meet criteria for a clinical concern. Avoid casual diagnosis while still addressing moral and relational harm.'},{question:'What if someone keeps relapsing?',response:'Relapse should not be normalized as harmless, but neither should it become proof of hopelessness. Review triggers, strengthen barriers, deepen accountability, seek counseling if patterns persist, and continue repentance without secrecy.'},{question:'Should a spouse be told?',response:'Honesty is generally important in covenant relationships, but disclosure may need wise pastoral or therapeutic guidance, especially where safety, coercion, trauma, or severe conflict is involved.'}],misunderstandings:['Pornography is only a male problem.','Shame is the best way to motivate change.','Marriage automatically cures pornography use.','Every person who struggles is clinically addicted.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Move devices out of high-risk private settings.','Use accountability that asks specific, non-shaming questions.'],family:['Use age-appropriate filters and open conversation.','Teach children what to do if explicit content appears unexpectedly.'],church:['Offer confidential pathways for help with safeguarding limits clearly explained.','Address exploitation and consent, not only personal purity.'],community:['Support anti-exploitation and trafficking work.','Know how to report nonconsensual sexual images or child sexual abuse material appropriately.']}});

A({week:25,title:'Marriage Redefined?',summary:'Christian marriage is a covenant of faithful, self-giving love. Historic Christian and Nazarene teaching understands marriage as the union of a man and a woman, but the church must teach that conviction together with mutuality, protection from abuse, and grace toward every person.',scripture:['Genesis 2:18–25','Matthew 19:3–9','Ephesians 5:21–33','Hebrews 13:4'],memoryVerse:'Ephesians 5:21',bigIdea:'Biblical marriage is not simply romance, legal status, or cultural tradition. It is a covenant calling spouses to fidelity, mutual self-giving, truth, forgiveness, responsibility, and love shaped by Christ.',objectives:['Read Jesus’ teaching about marriage in its creation and divorce context.','Understand marriage as covenant rather than ownership.','Interpret Ephesians 5 through Christlike self-giving and mutual submission.','Reject abuse, coercion, and domination as distortions of marriage.','Speak Christian conviction about marriage without contempt for people who disagree.'],openingQuestion:'What would change if Christians were known first for the quality of our marriages before we were known for arguments about the definition of marriage?',sections:[{heading:'Jesus Returns to Creation',type:'scripture',content:'In Matthew 19 Jesus answers a question about divorce by returning to Genesis. He presents marriage as a one-flesh covenant rooted in male and female creation and emphasizes its seriousness rather than treating spouses as disposable.'},{heading:'Covenant Is More Than Contract',type:'teaching',content:'A contract mainly protects an exchange; covenant language emphasizes faithful belonging and responsibility. Marriage includes promises that remain meaningful when feelings fluctuate.'},{heading:'Ephesians Centers the Marriage on Christ',type:'christ',content:'Ephesians 5 begins the household section with mutual submission and gives husbands the model of Christ’s self-giving love. Whatever one’s interpretation of marital roles, domination, intimidation, humiliation, and violence contradict the character of Jesus.'},{heading:'Marriage Is Not the Measure of Human Worth',type:'clarity',content:'Jesus was single, Paul commends singleness, and the church is family for married and unmarried believers. Elevating marriage must never mean treating single, divorced, widowed, or celibate people as incomplete.'},{heading:'Conviction Does Not Require Contempt',type:'discernment',content:'Christians living in pluralistic societies will encounter legal definitions and relationships that differ from church teaching. We can maintain doctrine while treating same-sex couples and everyone else with honesty, courtesy, neighbor-love, and equal human dignity.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'If married, ask your spouse where your love needs to become more sacrificial, attentive, or truthful. If single, identify one way you can strengthen another person’s covenant faithfulness or build healthy Christian community.'}],questions:['Why does Jesus return to Genesis in Matthew 19?','What is the difference between covenant and convenience?','How should Christ’s self-giving shape power inside marriage?','Why is abuse incompatible with Christian marriage?','How can the church honor marriage without treating singleness as second-class?','How can conviction be communicated without contempt?'],prayer:'God of covenant faithfulness, strengthen marriages in truth, tenderness, fidelity, forgiveness, and courage. Protect people from abuse and coercion. Help the church honor both marriage and singleness, and teach our convictions with the grace and dignity of Jesus. Amen.',leaderReminder:'Do not turn this lesson into a debate about same-sex couples while ignoring the condition of Christian marriages. Teach doctrine, but make covenant faithfulness, abuse prevention, and Christlike love central.',background:[{heading:'Genesis one-flesh union',content:'Genesis 2 presents marriage as kinship-forming union marked by leaving, cleaving, and one-flesh life.'},{heading:'Jesus and divorce',content:'Matthew 19 occurs within a debate about permissible divorce. Jesus protects the seriousness of covenant rather than giving husbands easy permission to discard wives.'},{heading:'Ephesians household teaching',content:'Paul speaks within a patriarchal Greco-Roman household world while radically centering marital conduct on Christ’s sacrificial love and the church’s submission to Christ.'}],theology:['Covenant: marriage involves enduring promises and responsibility.','Creation: historic Christian teaching grounds marriage in male-female one-flesh union.','Christology: marital power is judged by self-giving love.','Fidelity: sexual exclusivity and truthfulness matter.','Church: married and single believers belong equally to the household of God.'],worldview:{foundation:'Historic Christian and Nazarene teaching defines marriage as covenant union between a man and a woman under God.',affirm:'Civil society includes people with different convictions, and Christians can protect religious freedom while also treating neighbors fairly and respectfully.',caution:'Marriage doctrine can be distorted into culture-war rhetoric while adultery, neglect, coercion, abuse, and lovelessness inside Christian marriages receive less attention.',response:'Teach the church’s covenant vision, strengthen marriages, protect the vulnerable, honor singleness, and treat all neighbors with dignity.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'How should Christians respond to legal same-sex marriage?',response:'Churches may maintain their theological definition of marriage while recognizing that civil law can define marriage differently. Christians should navigate legal and civic life without harassment, dehumanization, or dishonesty.'},{question:'Does submission require remaining in abuse?',response:'No. Abuse contradicts Christlike love and may require separation, safety planning, law enforcement, professional support, church discipline, or other protective action. Submission is never permission for violence or coercive control.'},{question:'What about divorce?',response:'Scripture treats divorce as serious and addresses circumstances such as sexual unfaithfulness and abandonment; churches differ on some applications. Pastoral care should consider safety, truth, repentance, legal realities, and the whole biblical witness rather than issuing casual judgments.'}],misunderstandings:['Biblical marriage gives one spouse permission to dominate the other.','Defending marriage requires demeaning people in same-sex relationships.','Marriage is necessary for a complete Christian life.','Forgiveness requires a spouse to remain in danger.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['If married, ask whether your spouse experiences your love as safe and self-giving.','Refuse jokes that normalize contempt between husbands and wives.'],family:['Teach covenant, consent, fidelity, and conflict repair.','Support family members without making marital status their worth.'],church:['Maintain clear abuse-reporting and safety practices.','Offer marriage preparation and support before crises occur.'],community:['Treat couples and families outside church teaching with courtesy and fairness.','Protect the church’s freedom to teach its doctrine without using that freedom to justify mistreatment.']}});

A({week:26,title:'Single, Faithful, and Fulfilled',summary:'Jesus and Paul both dignify singleness. Christian completeness is found in Christ, not marital status, and the church should become genuine family rather than treating single adults as people waiting for real life to begin.',scripture:['1 Corinthians 7:7–8, 25–35','Matthew 19:10–12','Isaiah 56:3–5','Mark 3:31–35'],memoryVerse:'1 Corinthians 7:35',bigIdea:'Singleness can involve freedom, grief, calling, waiting, desire, contentment, or several at once. The gospel does not require pretending loneliness is unreal, but it refuses the claim that marriage is necessary for a meaningful or holy life.',objectives:['Recognize singleness as honored in the New Testament.','Reject the idea that marriage completes a person.','Make room for both contentment and honest longing.','Identify ways churches unintentionally marginalize single adults.','Strengthen Christian community as real family.'],openingQuestion:'What messages do churches send when nearly every example of adult maturity assumes marriage and children?',sections:[{heading:'Paul Calls Singleness a Gift, Not a Defect',type:'scripture',content:'First Corinthians 7 does not command everyone to remain single, but Paul clearly treats unmarried life as capable of focused faithfulness. His teaching challenges any church culture that assumes marriage is spiritually superior.'},{heading:'Jesus Lived a Fully Human Single Life',type:'christ',content:'Christian theology cannot call singleness incomplete without confronting the life of Jesus. He formed deep friendships, lived in community, welcomed children, served sacrificially, and fulfilled His calling without marriage.'},{heading:'Contentment and Longing Can Coexist',type:'clarity',content:'A single person may be deeply fulfilled and still desire marriage. Another may not desire marriage. Widowed, divorced, never-married, and celibate people have different stories. Churches should avoid one-size-fits-all explanations.'},{heading:'The Church Is Supposed to Be Family',type:'teaching',content:'Jesus expands family language around those who do God’s will. Christian community should include shared meals, practical support, intergenerational friendship, holidays, caregiving, and belonging rather than making nuclear households the only real unit.'},{heading:'Sexual Holiness Applies in Every Status',type:'discernment',content:'Singleness does not make desire disappear, and marriage does not remove temptation. All believers need friendship, accountability, boundaries, grace, and a sexual ethic rooted in love and holiness.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'If single, identify one relationship or calling you want to invest in rather than waiting for a different life to begin. If married, invite a single person into ordinary friendship without making them a project or trying to fix their status.'}],questions:['What positive value does Paul see in singleness?','Why does Jesus challenge the idea that marriage completes a person?','How can contentment coexist with desire for marriage?','Where does church life unintentionally center married families only?','What does real spiritual family look like beyond Sunday services?','How can married and single believers share life more deeply?'],prayer:'Jesus, You are enough to give every life meaning and calling. Comfort loneliness, honor desire, strengthen contentment, and deliver the church from treating marital status as spiritual rank. Make us a real family where married and single people are known, needed, and loved. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Corinthian marriage questions',content:'First Corinthians 7 responds to questions about marriage, sexual relations, divorce, engagement, and singleness in a complex church setting.'},{heading:'Singleness in the ancient world',content:'Marriage carried major economic, family, and social expectations, making the New Testament’s honor of celibate singleness striking.'},{heading:'Jesus and expanded family',content:'Jesus relativizes biological family as the sole center of belonging and creates a community organized around discipleship.'}],theology:['Vocation: calling is not dependent on marriage.','Church: baptism creates genuine family bonds.','Chastity: sexual holiness applies to married and unmarried people.','Contentment: fulfillment is rooted in Christ rather than status.','Hospitality: Christian households can become places of wider belonging.'],worldview:{foundation:'Marriage is good, but it is not the final human destiny; resurrection life is centered in God’s kingdom.',affirm:'Friendship, vocation, service, creativity, extended family, community, and spiritual kinship can carry profound meaning.',caution:'Churches can idolize the nuclear family, treat singles as immature, or use matchmaking as a substitute for friendship.',response:'Honor singleness, support honest longing, create real community, and invite every believer into meaningful responsibility.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is singleness always a spiritual gift someone enjoys?',response:'Not necessarily. Paul’s language can describe a grace for faithful living, but some people experience singleness as chosen, others as unwanted, and many as mixed. Do not demand cheerful denial of grief.'},{question:'Should single Christians date?',response:'Dating can be a wise path toward marriage when pursued with honesty, boundaries, shared faith, and realistic discernment. Singleness should not be treated merely as a waiting room, however.'},{question:'What about divorced or widowed adults?',response:'Their needs and possibilities differ. The church should honor grief, avoid assumptions, provide practical community, and offer careful pastoral guidance regarding future relationships.'}],misunderstandings:['Marriage is evidence of greater maturity.','Single adults need to be fixed or matched.','Contentment means never wanting marriage.','Family ministry should focus almost entirely on couples with children.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Build a life of friendship, service, and vocation now.','Do not postpone spiritual growth until relationship status changes.'],family:['Include single relatives and friends in ordinary family rhythms without pity.','Avoid intrusive questions about dating.'],church:['Include single adults in leadership and teaching examples.','Design events and care systems that do not assume everyone attends as a couple.'],community:['Support housing, caregiving, and social networks that recognize non-married households.','Value friendship as a serious social bond.']}});

A({week:27,title:'Raising Kids in a Confused Culture',summary:'Deuteronomy 6 places discipleship in ordinary life. Parents and churches form children not mainly through fear of culture but through practiced love of God, honest conversation, wise boundaries, and consistent example.',scripture:['Deuteronomy 6:4–9','Psalm 78:1–7','Ephesians 6:1–4','Mark 10:13–16'],memoryVerse:'Deuteronomy 6:6–7',bigIdea:'Children need more than rules about what Christians oppose. They need adults whose lives show why Jesus is trustworthy, who welcome questions, repair mistakes, teach discernment, and create age-appropriate boundaries.',objectives:['See discipleship as a daily relational practice.','Shift from fear-based parenting toward formation in love and wisdom.','Recognize the power of adult example.','Practice age-appropriate conversation about difficult cultural issues.','Build one repeatable family or church discipleship rhythm.'],openingQuestion:'If children remember our emotional tone more than our rules, what are we teaching them about following Jesus?',sections:[{heading:'Teach in the Rhythm of Ordinary Life',type:'scripture',content:'Deuteronomy 6 places God’s words in sitting, walking, lying down, and rising. Formation happens through repeated ordinary moments, not only formal lessons or crisis conversations.'},{heading:'Model Before You Lecture',type:'teaching',content:'Children notice how adults handle anger, money, technology, disagreement, apologies, neighbors, work, and worship. A rule contradicted by adult behavior teaches hypocrisy more effectively than the rule teaches holiness.'},{heading:'Questions Are Not the Enemy of Faith',type:'clarity',content:'Children and teenagers need permission to ask difficult questions without being mocked or panicked over. Honest questions can become opportunities to model how Christians seek truth rather than fear it.'},{heading:'Boundaries Should Grow Toward Wisdom',type:'discernment',content:'Young children need strong external boundaries. As they mature, discipleship should increasingly explain why boundaries exist and train internal judgment. Control that never develops discernment leaves young adults unprepared.'},{heading:'Jesus Welcomes Children as People',type:'christ',content:'Jesus receives children rather than treating them as interruptions or future adults. Christian formation respects their dignity now, protects them from harm, and refuses to use fear, humiliation, or coercion as spiritual tools.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Choose one daily rhythm—meal, drive, bedtime, morning, or weekly outing—and add a simple practice: one question, one Scripture, one gratitude, or one prayer. Keep it sustainable rather than impressive.'}],questions:['What does Deuteronomy 6 assume about where formation happens?','What are children learning from adult behavior that we may not intend?','Why can fear-based parenting produce fragile faith?','How should boundaries change as children mature?','What questions do children need permission to ask?','What small rhythm could your family or church sustain?'],prayer:'Father, help us form the next generation with truth, patience, courage, and love. Forgive the ways our example contradicts our words. Give children safe places to ask questions and adults humble enough to say “I do not know” and keep learning with them. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Deuteronomy and covenant memory',content:'Israel’s families were responsible for passing on the story of God’s deliverance and commands through ordinary life and communal practice.'},{heading:'Psalm 78 and testimony',content:'The psalm connects generational teaching with remembering God’s works so children place their trust in God rather than merely inherit rules.'},{heading:'Ephesians and parental power',content:'Ephesians 6 addresses children and then limits parental authority by warning fathers not to provoke children while nurturing them in the Lord.'}],theology:['Formation: discipleship grows through habits, stories, relationships, and practices.','Grace: children need correction without identity-destroying shame.','Agency: maturity involves growing capacity for wise choice.','Community: parents are primary but should not disciple alone.','Safeguarding: spiritual formation never excuses abuse or coercion.'],worldview:{foundation:'Children are image-bearers entrusted to adults for nurture, protection, teaching, and increasing maturity.',affirm:'Schools, coaches, media, peers, extended family, churches, and communities can contribute positively to formation when parents remain engaged.',caution:'Fear can produce secrecy, brittle certainty, rebellion, or dependence on parental control instead of mature faith.',response:'Build relationship, model repentance, teach Scripture in context, explain boundaries, welcome questions, and strengthen discernment over time.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'How much should parents shield children from culture?',response:'Protection should be age-appropriate and gradually paired with guided exposure and discussion. The goal is not permanent insulation but mature discernment and safety.'},{question:'What if my child questions Christian teaching?',response:'Listen before reacting. Ask what they mean, explore the source of the question, answer what you can, study what you cannot, and keep the relationship open. Panic can teach children that faith cannot survive questions.'},{question:'What if parents disagree about faith or rules?',response:'Avoid putting children in the middle. Seek calm adult conversation, clarify shared values where possible, and pursue pastoral or family support if conflict is persistent.'}],misunderstandings:['Good Christian parenting guarantees adult children will believe.','More control always produces more holiness.','Questions are evidence of rebellion.','Parents must have immediate answers to every issue.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Apologize to a child when you are wrong.','Let children see your own Scripture reading and prayer as real practices.'],family:['Create a repeatable conversation rhythm.','Explain the reason behind boundaries as children mature.'],church:['Equip parents without shaming those whose children struggle.','Give young people safe, screened leaders and serious space for questions.'],community:['Partner with schools and youth organizations where appropriate.','Advocate for child safety and family support rather than only criticizing culture.']}});

A({week:28,title:'Freedom, Tolerance, and Conviction',summary:'Christian conviction does not require coercion, and respect does not require agreement. Believers can defend conscience and religious freedom while extending the same human dignity and civic fairness to people who believe differently.',scripture:['1 Peter 3:13–17','Romans 14:1–13','Acts 17:16–34','Matthew 7:12'],memoryVerse:'1 Peter 3:15–16',bigIdea:'The gospel is persuaded and embodied, not imposed by contempt. Christians can speak clearly, protect conscience, listen carefully, and live peacefully with neighbors whose beliefs and moral conclusions differ.',objectives:['Distinguish tolerance, approval, respect, and coercion.','Practice giving reasons for hope with gentleness.','Recognize conscience as important but not infallible.','Defend religious freedom consistently rather than only for Christians.','Choose one way to disagree without dehumanizing.'],openingQuestion:'Can you respect someone’s freedom to make a choice you believe is wrong without pretending you approve of the choice?',sections:[{heading:'Conviction and Gentleness Belong Together',type:'scripture',content:'First Peter tells believers to be ready to give a reason for their hope, but explicitly joins that defense to gentleness, respect, and a clear conscience. Harshness is not evidence of stronger truth.'},{heading:'Tolerance Is Not the Same as Agreement',type:'clarity',content:'In a pluralistic society, tolerance can mean allowing people legal and social space to hold beliefs or make choices we reject. Approval says the belief or choice is good. Respect says the person retains dignity. These categories should not be confused.'},{heading:'Conscience Needs Humility',type:'discernment',content:'Romans 14 values conscience in disputed matters while warning believers not to despise or judge one another. Conscience can be sincere and still require formation by Scripture, truth, and love.'},{heading:'Paul Understands Before He Argues',type:'christ',content:'In Acts 17 Paul observes Athens carefully and speaks from points his listeners understand before challenging their worldview. Faithful witness takes other people seriously enough to listen and respond accurately.'},{heading:'Freedom Should Be Defended Consistently',type:'worldview',content:'If Christians want freedom to worship, speak, organize, and live by conscience, we should understand why those protections matter for neighbors with different religions or no religion as well, within laws that protect public safety and equal rights.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Have one conversation with someone you disagree with. Before stating your position, summarize theirs in a way they recognize as fair. Then explain your conviction without insulting motives or demanding agreement.'}],questions:['How does 1 Peter describe the manner of Christian defense?','What is the difference between tolerance and approval?','Can a sincere conscience be mistaken?','Why does Paul pay attention to Athenian beliefs before challenging them?','Should Christians defend religious freedom for other religions too?','What disagreement do you need to handle with greater fairness?'],prayer:'Lord Jesus, give us courage without coercion and gentleness without fear. Form our consciences by truth. Help us defend freedom fairly, represent others accurately, listen before speaking, and treat every neighbor with the dignity we want for ourselves. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Minority Christians in 1 Peter',content:'First Peter addresses believers navigating suspicion and hostility without political dominance, emphasizing honorable conduct and patient witness.'},{heading:'Romans 14 disputes',content:'Paul addresses food and sacred-day disagreements among believers, requiring mutual restraint without declaring all moral questions indifferent.'},{heading:'Paul at Athens',content:'Acts 17 models contextual engagement: observation, respectful connection, clear challenge, and freedom of response.'}],theology:['Conscience: believers are accountable to God and must keep learning.','Witness: truth is offered through persuasion and example.','Dignity: disagreement does not remove neighbor status.','Freedom: coerced faith is not genuine discipleship.','Justice: protections should be applied consistently.'],worldview:{foundation:'Truth matters, but human beings should not be coerced into faith by humiliation or unequal dignity.',affirm:'Pluralistic societies can create space for deep disagreement while protecting worship, speech, association, and conscience.',caution:'“Tolerance” can be used to silence conviction, while “religious freedom” can be used selectively to seek privilege rather than equal liberty.',response:'Defend conscience consistently, obey just laws, challenge injustice, persuade rather than demean, and extend the fairness you seek.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Does tolerance mean every belief is equally true?',response:'No. Tolerance addresses how people live with disagreement, not whether all claims are equally accurate or moral.'},{question:'Should religious freedom protect beliefs I find offensive?',response:'Generally, freedom is meaningful precisely when it protects unpopular beliefs, subject to laws protecting people from violence, abuse, fraud, and other concrete harms.'},{question:'What if conscience conflicts with law?',response:'Christians should take law seriously, seek lawful accommodations where possible, distinguish inconvenience from genuine moral conflict, and accept that civil disobedience may carry consequences when obedience to God truly requires it.'}],misunderstandings:['Respect requires agreement.','Conviction requires making others follow Christian rules.','Conscience can never be wrong.','Religious freedom matters only when Christians benefit.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Practice accurately summarizing an opposing view.','Remove motive-reading from disagreement.'],family:['Teach children how to disagree without ridicule.','Explain why neighbors have freedom of belief.'],church:['Train members to understand religious-liberty principles beyond slogans.','Make room for good-faith disagreement on secondary issues.'],community:['Support equal treatment of different religious communities.','Build relationships with neighbors across belief differences without hiding your own faith.']}});

A({week:29,title:'War and Peace',summary:'Jesus blesses peacemakers in a world where nations still use force. Christians have long wrestled with pacifism, just-war reasoning, defense of the innocent, and the moral limits of military power.',scripture:['Matthew 5:9, 38–48','Romans 12:17–21','Romans 13:1–7','Isaiah 2:1–4'],memoryVerse:'Matthew 5:9',bigIdea:'The church should never make war feel holy or easy. Whatever position Christians take on military force, disciples of Jesus must love enemies, restrain vengeance, protect human dignity, seek peace, and judge force by serious moral limits.',objectives:['Understand Jesus’ call to peacemaking and enemy-love.','Recognize historic Christian disagreement about war.','Distinguish personal vengeance from public protection.','Apply moral limits such as necessity, proportionality, and protection of noncombatants.','Practice peacemaking before conflict becomes violence.'],openingQuestion:'What would change if Christians began every discussion of war by grieving the lives that will be lost rather than asking which side we support?',sections:[{heading:'Jesus Blesses Peacemakers',type:'scripture',content:'Matthew 5 places peacemaking inside the character of God’s kingdom and extends love even toward enemies. No Christian theory of war can simply set those commands aside.'},{heading:'The New Testament Also Recognizes Public Authority',type:'clarity',content:'Romans 13 describes governing authority as having a role in restraining wrongdoing. Christians disagree about whether and how this authorizes believers to participate in lethal force, but the distinction between personal revenge and public responsibility matters.'},{heading:'Two Major Christian Traditions',type:'teaching',content:'Some Christians believe Jesus’ teaching requires nonviolence and conscientious refusal of war. Others developed just-war reasoning to limit when force may be used and how. Both traditions should be represented fairly rather than caricatured.'},{heading:'Moral Limits Matter Even in a Just Cause',type:'discernment',content:'Claims of self-defense or justice do not make every tactic acceptable. Christian moral reasoning asks about legitimate authority, just cause, last resort, proportionality, discrimination between combatants and civilians, and realistic prospects of protecting life.'},{heading:'The Cross Exposes the Logic of Domination',type:'christ',content:'Jesus defeats sin not by seizing imperial power but through self-giving love and resurrection. The cross does not answer every policy question automatically, but it prevents Christians from glorifying domination, vengeance, or national power as the kingdom of God.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Pray for civilians, soldiers, leaders, refugees, wounded people, prisoners, and enemies in one current conflict without turning prayer into propaganda. Support one credible peace, humanitarian, refugee, or veteran-care effort.'}],questions:['What does Jesus mean by peacemaking?','How are personal vengeance and public responsibility different?','What is the strongest concern of Christian pacifism?','What is the strongest concern of just-war reasoning?','Why are noncombatant protection and proportionality essential moral questions?','How can churches pray during war without baptizing nationalism?'],prayer:'Prince of Peace, have mercy on a violent world. Protect civilians, restrain leaders, comfort the grieving, guide soldiers and peacemakers, and expose propaganda and hatred. Give Your church courage to seek justice without worshiping force and to love enemies without ignoring evil. Amen.',background:[{heading:'Early Christian practice',content:'Early Christians often displayed strong nonviolent instincts, though historical practice was varied and developed as Christians entered public office and military roles.'},{heading:'Just-war tradition',content:'Thinkers such as Augustine and Aquinas developed criteria intended to restrain rather than celebrate war, asking when force could be morally permissible and how it must be limited.'},{heading:'Peace church traditions',content:'Anabaptist and other Christian traditions have maintained that Jesus’ enemy-love calls the church to nonviolence even when states use force.'}],theology:['Peace: God’s final future is reconciliation and the end of war.','Enemy-love: opponents remain neighbors and image-bearers.','Government: states have responsibilities for public order.','Conscience: Christians may reach different conclusions about military participation.','Hope: no nation or military victory is the kingdom of God.'],worldview:{foundation:'War is always morally grave and should never be treated casually, romantically, or as automatic proof of patriotism.',affirm:'Defense, diplomacy, deterrence, humanitarian protection, peacekeeping, nonviolent resistance, and conscientious objection all require serious moral evaluation.',caution:'Governments and movements can use propaganda, fear, religion, and selective facts to make violence appear inevitable or righteous.',response:'Seek peace first, verify claims, protect civilians, limit force, honor conscience, care for veterans and victims, and refuse hatred.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Can a Christian serve in the military?',response:'Many traditions say yes within moral limits; peace churches may counsel conscientious nonparticipation in lethal force. The church should honor serious conscience rather than treating either path casually.'},{question:'What if force is the only realistic way to protect innocent people?',response:'This is a central argument for just-war reasoning. Even then, force should be necessary, proportionate, discriminating, accountable, and aimed at restoring a just peace rather than revenge.'},{question:'Does loving enemies mean a nation cannot defend itself?',response:'Christians disagree. All should reject hatred and collective dehumanization while taking seriously both Jesus’ commands and the responsibility to protect vulnerable people.'}],misunderstandings:['Supporting troops requires supporting every war.','Opposing a war means disrespecting soldiers.','A just cause makes every tactic just.','Christian pacifism is simply cowardice or passivity.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Verify wartime claims before sharing them.','Pray for people on all sides without moral equivalence where clear aggression exists.'],family:['Discuss war without dehumanizing national or ethnic groups.','Support military families and conscientious objectors with dignity.'],church:['Avoid flags, rhetoric, or rituals that blur church allegiance with nation.','Provide pastoral care for veterans, refugees, and people carrying moral injury.'],community:['Support refugee and humanitarian relief.','Encourage diplomacy, civilian protection, and veteran mental-health care.']}});

A({week:30,title:'Persecution of Christians',summary:'Jesus prepares His followers for opposition, but not every criticism, lost privilege, or consequence is persecution. Faithful Christians learn to distinguish suffering for Christ from suffering caused by our own harmful behavior.',scripture:['2 Timothy 3:10–17','Matthew 5:10–12','1 Peter 4:12–16','Acts 4:13–31'],memoryVerse:'1 Peter 4:16',bigIdea:'Christians should stand courageously when faithfulness costs something, support genuinely persecuted believers, and refuse to label every disagreement as persecution. Suffering for Christ should produce courage and love, not resentment or exaggeration.',objectives:['Understand New Testament expectations of opposition.','Distinguish persecution from criticism or consequences for wrongdoing.','Learn from believers facing severe restrictions or violence.','Practice courage without retaliatory hatred.','Support persecuted Christians in prayer and practical ways.'],openingQuestion:'How can calling every inconvenience “persecution” make it harder to recognize believers who actually face imprisonment, violence, or severe discrimination?',sections:[{heading:'Jesus Does Not Promise Social Approval',type:'scripture',content:'Matthew 5 blesses people persecuted for righteousness and for allegiance to Jesus. Christian faithfulness can bring real social, economic, legal, or physical cost.'},{heading:'Peter Adds an Important Qualification',type:'clarity',content:'First Peter says suffering as a Christian is different from suffering for wrongdoing. If our cruelty, dishonesty, harassment, incompetence, or lawbreaking causes consequences, we should not automatically rename those consequences persecution.'},{heading:'Courage Is Not Retaliation',type:'christ',content:'In Acts 4 the church responds to threats by praying for boldness, not revenge. Their courage remains centered on speaking and living the gospel.'},{heading:'Remember the Global Church',type:'worldview',content:'Christians in different places face very different realities, including social exclusion, discriminatory laws, surveillance, church closures, imprisonment, violence, or death. Our language should honor the seriousness of their experience.'},{heading:'Religious Freedom Should Serve Mission, Not Entitlement',type:'discernment',content:'Christians can advocate for freedom of worship, speech, conscience, and association. We should do so with consistency, also recognizing the dignity and lawful freedom of neighbors whose beliefs differ from ours.'},{heading:'Live It This Week',type:'challenge',content:'Learn about one Christian community facing serious persecution through a reputable source. Pray specifically, support trustworthy relief or advocacy if possible, and examine whether your own public speech uses persecution language accurately.'}],questions:['What kind of persecution does Jesus bless?','Why does Peter distinguish suffering for Christ from suffering for wrongdoing?','How does the church in Acts respond to threats?','What forms can persecution take besides physical violence?','Why should religious-freedom advocacy be consistent?','Where do you need greater courage, and where do you need greater humility?'],prayer:'Lord Jesus, strengthen believers who suffer because they belong to You. Protect the vulnerable, comfort families, open prison doors where You will, and give courage without hatred. Teach those of us with greater freedom to use it faithfully and to speak accurately about our own hardships. Amen.',background:[{heading:'New Testament minority communities',content:'Many early Christians lived without political power and faced local hostility, legal vulnerability, social pressure, or state violence at different times and places.'},{heading:'Peter on suffering',content:'First Peter repeatedly distinguishes honorable suffering from suffering caused by wrongdoing, protecting the church from self-righteous victimhood.'},{heading:'Acts and boldness',content:'When leaders threaten the apostles, the church prays for boldness and continues witness while trusting God with outcomes.'}],theology:['Witness: martyrdom language comes from the idea of bearing testimony.','Suffering: following Jesus can be costly.','Truth: Christians must describe opposition accurately.','Enemy-love: persecution never grants permission for hatred.','Freedom: public liberty can create space for mission and conscience.'],worldview:{foundation:'No government, movement, majority, or minority should persecute people for peaceful religious belief and practice.',affirm:'Religious-freedom law, diplomacy, humanitarian aid, documentation, asylum, and interfaith advocacy can protect vulnerable communities.',caution:'Persecution language can be inflated for political mobilization, while severe persecution elsewhere can be minimized or ignored.',response:'Stand firm, tell the truth, support the suffering, defend freedom consistently, and refuse retaliatory hatred.'},difficultQuestions:[{question:'Is being criticized for Christian beliefs persecution?',response:'Criticism can be painful and sometimes discriminatory, but disagreement alone is not necessarily persecution. Consider whether rights, safety, employment, legal status, worship, or equal treatment are actually being denied.'},{question:'What if a Christian faces consequences for refusing a job requirement on conscience grounds?',response:'The answer depends on law, role, harm, available accommodation, and the nature of the requirement. Seek legal and pastoral counsel rather than labeling every conflict the same way.'},{question:'Should Christians defend the religious freedom of other faiths?',response:'Yes, as a matter of consistent human dignity and conscience, subject to neutral laws protecting people from violence, abuse, fraud, and other concrete harm.'}],misunderstandings:['Any negative reaction to Christianity is persecution.','Suffering proves our behavior was righteous.','Persecuted Christians should respond with hatred toward opponents.','Religious freedom means Christians should receive special civic privilege.'],ministryApplications:{personal:['Use persecution language carefully.','Pray regularly for believers in high-risk settings.'],family:['Teach children the difference between disagreement and persecution.','Learn stories of faithful Christians across the world.'],church:['Support credible global church networks and relief organizations.','Prepare members for gracious witness rather than resentment.'],community:['Defend peaceful religious minorities from harassment.','Support lawful asylum and protection for people fleeing religious persecution.']}});
})();
